- What changes when moving to a 4-valued logic or alternative lattices?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with non-classical truth in a constrained system, using backtracking under Kleene semantics and additional operators (↔, ⊕) to reveal subtle propagation patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics and derived operators
- Backtracking with constraint propagation
- Handling composite operators like biconditional and XOR under 3-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function imp(a,b){ // a→b = (¬a) ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return (na==='T' || b==='T') ? 'T' : (na==='U' || b==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
}
function orb(a,b){ // max in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b;
}
function land(a,b){ // min in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a tiny 3-valued solver: propagation, clash detection, and controlled backtracking while preserving non-boolean placeholders.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued logic propagation
- Forward-checking and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking with Unknowns to preserve flexibility

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vars, constraints) {
  // vars: {S1: T|F|U, S2: T|F|U, S3: T|F|U}
  // constraints: list of functions returning {changed, ok}
  // Minimal sketch for illustration
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const c of constraints) {
      const res = c(vars);
      if (res.changed) changed = true;
      if (!res.ok) return {ok: false};
    }
  }
  return {ok: true, vars};
}
```

