## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about constraints in practical feature-flag settings and to design a solver using propagation and backtracking rather than pure search. It also exposes how to craft a constructive counterexample when needed.

## Key Concepts

- Constraint satisfaction with implications and disjunctions
- Unit propagation, backtracking, and a decision trail
- Real-world constraints in deployment feature flags and release gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal backtracker for boolean flags with given constraints
function sat(S){
  return (!S.S1 || S.S2) && (!S.S2 || S.S3) && (!S.S4 || !S.S1) && (S.S1 || S.S4) && (S.S2 || S.S4);
}
function satPartial(part){
  const v = (k)=> part.hasOwnProperty(k)? part[k] : null;
  if (v('S1')===true && v('S2')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===true && v('S3')===false) return false;
  if (v('S4')===true && v('S1')===true) return false;
  if (v('S1')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  return true;
}
function solve(partial={}, idx=0){
  const vars=['S1','S2','S3','S4'];
  if (!satPartial(partial)) return null;
  if (idx===vars.length) return partial;
  const name = vars[idx];
  for (const val of [false,true]){
    const next = Object.assign({}, partial, {[name]: val});
    const res = solve(next, idx+1);
    if (res) return res;
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(solve());
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to thousands of flags with mostly Horn clauses?
- What optimizations would you add for large constraint sets?

## Why This Is Asked
Every candidate demonstrates logic reasoning and basic constraint solving relevant to planning validations in software.

## Key Concepts
- Propositional logic and implications (A→B means ¬A ∨ B)
- Satisfiability with small variable sets
- Backtracking with constraint propagation for pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
function isSatisfiable(constraints, numVars = 3) {
  const vars = Array.from({length: numVars}, (_, i) => String.fromCharCode(65 + i));

  function backtrack(index, assignment) {
    if (index === numVars) return constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment));

    for (const val of [0, 1]) {
      assignment[vars[index]] = val;
      if (constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment))) {
        if (backtrack(index + 1, assignment)) return true;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }

  return backtrack(0, {});
}

const constraints = [
  a => a.A === 0 || a.B === 1,
  a => a.B === 0 || a.C === 1,
  a => a.A === 1 || a.C === 1
];
console.log(isSatisfiable(constraints)); // true
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to thousands of variables with CNF clauses?
- What are the trade-offs of backtracking vs. SAT solvers in practice?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests reasoning with Kleene 3-valued logic in interdependent constraints, and the ability to design a propagation/backtracking solver. It also requires concrete verification on a small example.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics for ∧, ∨, ¬, →, ↔
- Constraint propagation and backtracking with U values
